WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:01.070
Maximilien Robespierre

00:00:01.070 --> 00:00:05.330
Maximilien Robespierre promised to usher a
fairer, more representative form of government

00:00:05.330 --> 00:00:06.400
to the French people.

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:12.590
What they got was a reign of terror that saw
thousands facing the horror of the guillotine.

00:00:12.590 --> 00:00:15.330
Among Robespierre’s victims were the king
and queen of France.

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:20.380
When justice finally came it was a swift as
the slice of a blade.

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:30.960
In this week’s Biographics, we wade into
the terror with Maximilien Robespierre.

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:42.840
The Early Robespierre

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:47.230
Maximilien Robespierre entered the world on
May 6th, 1758.

00:00:47.230 --> 00:00:51.980
He was born in Arras, France though historians
have suspected for centuries that his family

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:53.469
originated from Ireland.

00:00:53.469 --> 00:00:58.179
By the time that Max was born, however, they
had been French citizens for many generations.

00:00:58.179 --> 00:01:03.070
The child was conceived out of wedlock but
by the time he was born his parents had married.

00:01:03.070 --> 00:01:08.590
Like his own father before him, Max’s father
was a lawyer, but not a very successful one.

00:01:08.590 --> 00:01:12.740
This left the family with a constant debt
hanging over its head.

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:17.000
Things didn’t get any easier for the Robespierre’s
when Max’s mother died giving birth to a

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:19.470
sibling when he was six years old.

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:24.500
Looking after four children was too much for
Robespierre senior, so his offspring were

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:26.320
divided among his relatives.

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:31.080
His mother’s death had a profound effect
upon young Max.

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:34.270
No longer was he the carefree child of old.

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:35.931
Now he was sullen and serious.

00:01:35.931 --> 00:01:41.890
He also applied himself diligently to his
schooling as if drowning his grief in his

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:42.890
studies.

00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:47.399
When he was eleven years of age, young Robespierre
was awarded a scholarship to the Lycee-Louis-le-Grand

00:01:47.399 --> 00:01:49.040
in Paris.

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:53.680
He would continue studying there for the next
twelve years, emerging at age twenty-three

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:54.790
with a law degree.

00:01:54.790 --> 00:01:59.060
As well as law, he also studied literature,
rhetoric and the classics.

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:01.930
Life at the prestigious school was very structured.

00:02:01.930 --> 00:02:06.509
Formerly a Jesuit institution, it was now
under the control of the University of Paris.

00:02:06.509 --> 00:02:10.080
The day began and ended with formal prayers
and bible study.

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:14.260
The school also had an excellent library,
which Robespierre made liberal use of.

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:18.510
The most well-known incident arising from
Robespierre’s time at the school occurred

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:19.970
when he was seventeen.

00:02:19.970 --> 00:02:24.860
His excellent oratory skills led to him being
selected to give a speech before King Louis

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:27.840
XVI and Queen Marie Antoinette.

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:33.080
He perfected his wording and practised his
delivery only to be snubbed by the royal couple

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:36.020
who never even bothered to get out of their
carriage.

00:02:36.020 --> 00:02:40.440
It was a personal violation that he would
never forget.

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:45.520
During his time at the Lycee, Robespierre
was also exposed to enlightenment philosophy,

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:48.510
especially the works of Jean-Jacques Rousseau.

00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:53.200
Rousseau was a powerful advocate for a more
democratic form of government coupled with

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:54.420
social empowerment.

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:59.400
However, Robespierre was not able to read
Rousseau in the Lycee’s library.

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:04.070
His works were considered to be dangerous
and so copies of his famous discourse, published

00:03:04.070 --> 00:03:06.700
twenty years earlier, had to be smuggled in.

00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:12.710
In his later life, Robespierre would label
his later years at the Lycee as a nursery

00:03:12.710 --> 00:03:14.320
for republicanism.

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:19.460
By the time he had reached his early twenties,
Robespierre was a vocal advocate for natural

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:20.460
rights.

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:24.400
He championed the rights of the underprivileged,
speaking at every public opportunity.

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:29.320
In fact, he was such an enthusiastic champion
of basic human rights that he became physically

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:32.100
exhausted to the point of collapse.

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:35.390
Robespierre proved to be an unstoppable force
of nature.

00:03:35.390 --> 00:03:40.520
This led to him becoming a familiar and well-known
figure in and around his home-town of Arras.

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:43.810
In the mid-1780’s he joined the Academy
of Arras.

00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:49.110
His first speech before the Academy was part
of a competition and shone a spotlight upon

00:03:49.110 --> 00:03:51.300
the lack of morality in politics.

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:56.140
It didn’t win first prize, but he was rewarded
with a large cash prize.

00:03:56.140 --> 00:04:00.370
This whet his appetite and over the next few
years he entered a number of essay and poetry

00:04:00.370 --> 00:04:01.370
competitions.

00:04:01.370 --> 00:04:06.000
He also joined an elite literary society known
as the Rosatia Club.

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:10.690
Since graduating from the Lycee, Robespierre
had established a modest law practice.

00:04:10.690 --> 00:04:14.260
From the start he began taking on cases that
were controversial.

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:21.360
In 1789, he took on the state in a case that
directly challenged the notion of lettres-des-cachet,

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:23.570
or imprisonment without trial.

00:04:23.570 --> 00:04:27.560
During the course of the trial he actually
wrote to the king and personally requested

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:30.580
his assistance in getting rid of this abuse.

00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:41.690
On the Brink

00:04:41.690 --> 00:04:45.120
By the 1780’s France was desperately running
out of money.

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:49.190
They had spent a lot of money in assisting
the Americans in the previous decade.

00:04:49.190 --> 00:04:53.560
This was compounded by a lavish amount of
spending on the part of the monarchy.

00:04:53.560 --> 00:04:58.820
The appointment of a succession of finance
ministers to try to turn around the country’s

00:04:58.820 --> 00:05:00.840
flagging economy had little effect.

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:05.460
By the end of the decade there was a growing
call for a meeting of the Estates General,

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:08.070
representing the clergy, the nobility and
the people.

00:05:08.070 --> 00:05:13.580
Meanwhile, the King unilaterally enacted a
series of laws to fill the royal coffers.

00:05:13.580 --> 00:05:18.300
This included the raising of taxes and the
cutting of spending on essential services.

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:22.810
The following day the French parliament condemned
the king’s actions, labelling the raising

00:05:22.810 --> 00:05:24.560
of taxes as illegal.

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:27.930
The king’s response was to exile the parliament.

00:05:27.930 --> 00:05:32.030
This led to growing public protests in Paris.

00:05:32.030 --> 00:05:36.280
In response to this desperate situation, a
new finance minister was put in place.

00:05:36.280 --> 00:05:39.780
This was Etienne-Charles de Loménie de Brienne.

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:44.810
He proposed a new five-year plan which was
designed to restore French credit as well

00:05:44.810 --> 00:05:49.560
as presenting a full accounting of the French
government’s finances to whoever wanted

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:50.560
to see it.

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:55.740
He called for the return of the Parisian parliament
after their normal autumn break.

00:05:55.740 --> 00:06:01.540
The parliament reconvened on November 17,
1787 in the rare presence of the king.

00:06:01.540 --> 00:06:05.730
After an 8-hour debate the parliament failed
to authorise Brienne’s five-year plan.

00:06:05.730 --> 00:06:11.910
However, King Louis XVI went ahead and authorised
the loans needed to restore credit anyway.

00:06:11.910 --> 00:06:14.560
The king left the chamber but the debate continued.

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:18.940
It was resolved that the parliament would
officially condemn the king’s action.

00:06:18.940 --> 00:06:22.880
The following day the leading members of the
parliament were exiled by the king.

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:27.110
In response to this, provincial parliaments
across the country began to refuse to register

00:06:27.110 --> 00:06:31.690
laws as a protest to the apparently despotic
actions of the king.

00:06:31.690 --> 00:06:38.270
On May 3, 1788 the Parliament of Paris issued
a declaration on the fundamental laws of the

00:06:38.270 --> 00:06:39.270
realm.

00:06:39.270 --> 00:06:43.841
It included the right of parliament to register
new laws, the role of the Estates General

00:06:43.841 --> 00:06:47.840
and the freedom of all subjects from arbitrary
arrest.

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:52.730
Despite this, the leaders of the parliament
were taken into custody the following day.

00:06:52.730 --> 00:06:57.260
A few days later the king issued a series
of judicial reforms which were designed to

00:06:57.260 --> 00:06:58.990
cement his absolute power.

00:06:58.990 --> 00:07:02.860
The reforms effectively neutralized the Parliament
of Paris.

00:07:02.860 --> 00:07:07.800
In response to this outrage, provincial parliaments
around the country refused to uphold any of

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:08.870
the government’s laws.

00:07:08.870 --> 00:07:15.200
France was now operating without any formal
justice system.

00:07:15.200 --> 00:07:22.919
France in Revolt

00:07:22.919 --> 00:07:26.010
The country was approaching widespread public
revolt.

00:07:26.010 --> 00:07:30.870
In an attempt to control the damage, Finance
minister Brienne, called for a sitting of

00:07:30.870 --> 00:07:35.180
the Estates General on May 1, 1789.

00:07:35.180 --> 00:07:38.510
Meanwhile the French government was completely
bankrupt.

00:07:38.510 --> 00:07:42.860
With no ideas to get the country out of the
red, Brienne was forced to resign and former

00:07:42.860 --> 00:07:46.280
Finance Minister Jacques Necker was put back
in office.

00:07:46.280 --> 00:07:51.180
Necker had the general confidence of the people
and managed to recall the parliaments around

00:07:51.180 --> 00:07:52.180
the country.

00:07:52.180 --> 00:07:56.710
The Paris parliament announced that the Estates
General would meet according to the historic

00:07:56.710 --> 00:08:01.520
precedent where the representation of the
people – the Third Estate – would be numerically

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:04.949
less than that of the clergy and the nobility.

00:08:04.949 --> 00:08:08.680
This was met with widespread public disapproval.

00:08:08.680 --> 00:08:13.650
Through long negotiations with the king, Necker
was able to announce in December, 1788 that

00:08:13.650 --> 00:08:18.010
the representation of the third estate would
be doubled in the Estates General.

00:08:18.010 --> 00:08:23.380
Meanwhile hundreds of pamphlets had been appearing
around Paris with titles such as ‘What is

00:08:23.380 --> 00:08:24.380
the 3rd Estate?’

00:08:24.380 --> 00:08:29.290
Rather than being comprised of peasants, workers
or artisans, the Third Estate was made up

00:08:29.290 --> 00:08:34.320
of lawyers and office holders, the well to
do who had enough time to engage in the slow

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:35.680
processes involved.

00:08:35.680 --> 00:08:40.200
The pamphleteers strongly criticized the power
of the clergy and the nobility and the lack

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:42.590
of representation of the masses.

00:08:42.590 --> 00:08:48.220
Several leaders arose among the Third Estate,
including Maximilien Robespierre.

00:08:48.220 --> 00:08:54.210
By 1788, Robespierre was positioning himself
to play a key role in the coming revolution.

00:08:54.210 --> 00:08:59.380
He participated in a series of debates regarding
the make-up of the Third Estate and the ratio

00:08:59.380 --> 00:09:02.250
of the three components of the Estates General.

00:09:02.250 --> 00:09:07.030
He published a pamphlet which addressed local
issues in Arras with the view of getting himself

00:09:07.030 --> 00:09:09.480
elected onto the Third Estate.

00:09:09.480 --> 00:09:15.670
In the pamphlet he strongly stressed two key
ideas; the importance of elected representation

00:09:15.670 --> 00:09:17.740
and concern for the poor.

00:09:17.740 --> 00:09:24.029
By now, Robespierre had a clearly defined
notion of who the enemy was – the clergy

00:09:24.029 --> 00:09:25.210
and the nobility.

00:09:25.210 --> 00:09:30.550
In March, 1789 he was elected as a representative
from Arras to the Third Estate.

00:09:30.550 --> 00:09:33.880
He was chosen to participate in the drafting
of a list of grievances.

00:09:33.880 --> 00:09:38.880
At the same time, he pushed for new initiatives
that would give the lower classes access to

00:09:38.880 --> 00:09:40.339
the political system.

00:09:40.339 --> 00:09:43.960
Robespierre’s second pamphlet was a foretaste
of things to come.

00:09:43.960 --> 00:09:58.130
It was called ‘the Enemies of the Country
Unmasked.’

00:09:58.130 --> 00:09:59.130
The Estates General

00:09:59.130 --> 00:10:02.649
The Estates General met on May 5, 1789 at
Versailles.

00:10:02.649 --> 00:10:06.410
Thirty-year-old Robespierre was one of eight
representatives from Arras.

00:10:06.410 --> 00:10:11.580
In the formal opening of proceedings, he and
his fellow Third Estate members refused to

00:10:11.580 --> 00:10:13.450
bow before the king.

00:10:13.450 --> 00:10:16.160
That first day, Robespierre began to stand
out.

00:10:16.160 --> 00:10:20.880
He was not an imposing physical figure and
his voice was less than inspiring.

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:26.230
But he dressed impeccably and had an amazing
ability to recall details.

00:10:26.230 --> 00:10:29.970
He customarily wore a powdered wig and a formal
waistcoat.

00:10:29.970 --> 00:10:34.779
In the first week of the assembly, he formed
a breakaway group, known as the Breton Club,

00:10:34.779 --> 00:10:39.980
which held their own meetings to discuss the
abolition of the privileges of the clergy

00:10:39.980 --> 00:10:41.260
and nobility.

00:10:41.260 --> 00:10:46.550
On June 7th, Robespierre gave a passionate
speech criticizing the excesses of the clergy.

00:10:46.550 --> 00:10:51.890
It was one of the major motivators for the
establishment of the National Assembly three

00:10:51.890 --> 00:10:53.010
days later.

00:10:53.010 --> 00:10:57.480
On that date the Third Estate sent messages
to the Clergy and Nobility requesting that

00:10:57.480 --> 00:11:01.370
they agree to common verification by a head
count.

00:11:01.370 --> 00:11:06.470
Receiving no response, they declared themselves
the only legitimate representative body renaming

00:11:06.470 --> 00:11:08.180
themselves the ‘Commons’.

00:11:08.180 --> 00:11:11.460
The public received this news with great enthusiasm.

00:11:11.460 --> 00:11:15.900
Eventually the clergy, under much public pressure,
joined the National Assembly.

00:11:15.900 --> 00:11:19.680
On the morning of June 20th, the National
Assembly turned up to their meeting place

00:11:19.680 --> 00:11:23.490
at Versailles to find the gates locked and
the entrance manned by guards.

00:11:23.490 --> 00:11:27.420
They quickly retreated to a nearby tennis
court on the grounds of Versailles.

00:11:27.420 --> 00:11:32.830
The members were enraged at the despotism
of the king in shutting out the National Assembly.

00:11:32.830 --> 00:11:37.339
They unanimously asserted what has become
known as the ‘Tennis Court Oath’ – they

00:11:37.339 --> 00:11:43.680
vowed to remain in session until ‘the constitution
of the Realm and public regeneration are established

00:11:43.680 --> 00:11:44.910
and assured.’

00:11:44.910 --> 00:11:47.840
On June 17th, the King opened the Royal Session.

00:11:47.840 --> 00:11:51.070
His first move was to declare the National
Assembly invalid.

00:11:51.070 --> 00:11:55.130
He then put forward a 35-point plan for reform.

00:11:55.130 --> 00:12:00.120
His final move was to announce that nothing
that the Estates general did was valid without

00:12:00.120 --> 00:12:02.880
his personal consent.

00:12:02.880 --> 00:12:11.610
A New National Assembly

00:12:11.610 --> 00:12:15.399
Once the king had dismissed the assembly,
the nobility and clerics filed out.

00:12:15.399 --> 00:12:19.660
But the members of the National Assembly,
comprising the Third Estate and the Clergy,

00:12:19.660 --> 00:12:20.750
remained where they were.

00:12:20.750 --> 00:12:25.110
It was declared that they would only leave
at the end of bayonets.

00:12:25.110 --> 00:12:29.220
With the entire country in support of the
National Assembly, the king backed down.

00:12:29.220 --> 00:12:34.070
He ordered the First and Second Estates to
join the National Assembly.

00:12:34.070 --> 00:12:36.010
Still the riots did not end.

00:12:36.010 --> 00:12:38.860
Louis sent troops to surround the city of
Paris.

00:12:38.860 --> 00:12:43.110
The national assembly now got to work and
hammered out a list of demands to put to the

00:12:43.110 --> 00:12:44.110
king.

00:12:44.110 --> 00:12:47.880
Robespierre was one of those who presented
them, with the first one being that he remove

00:12:47.880 --> 00:12:48.880
the troops.

00:12:48.880 --> 00:12:50.810
The king ignored the demand.

00:12:50.810 --> 00:12:55.339
By the beginning of July, there were 20,000
soldiers around the city.

00:12:55.339 --> 00:12:57.970
Robespierre responded by making the following
public statement . . .

00:12:57.970 --> 00:13:02.660
No one loves armed missionaries; the first
lesson of nature and prudence is to repulse

00:13:02.660 --> 00:13:04.790
them as necessary.

00:13:04.790 --> 00:13:06.029
And so it proved to be.

00:13:06.029 --> 00:13:11.450
On July 11th, the king dismissed finance minister
Necker, who was still publicly popular.

00:13:11.450 --> 00:13:13.390
This led to rage among the people.

00:13:13.390 --> 00:13:18.260
Two days later rumors spread like wildfire
that the French army was about to launch an

00:13:18.260 --> 00:13:19.430
attack on the people.

00:13:19.430 --> 00:13:25.630
A mob of citizens reacted to the impending
threat by seizing 28,000 rifles from a veteran’s

00:13:25.630 --> 00:13:26.630
hospital.

00:13:26.630 --> 00:13:28.350
They now needed gunpowder to use them.

00:13:28.350 --> 00:13:32.080
They found it at an unused prison in the city
called the Bastille.

00:13:32.080 --> 00:13:35.390
The guards tried to hold of the crowds but
then fired into them.

00:13:35.390 --> 00:13:37.860
Hundreds of people fell down dead.

00:13:37.860 --> 00:13:42.110
The now out of control mob overpowered and
killed the guards and then gained access to

00:13:42.110 --> 00:13:43.200
the gunpowder.

00:13:43.200 --> 00:13:47.740
When the King sent soldiers to bring order,
they switched loyalties and joined the people.

00:13:47.740 --> 00:13:52.440
Louis now knew that he could trust no one,
not even his protective army.

00:13:52.440 --> 00:13:55.200
Meanwhile, the National Assembly remained
in session.

00:13:55.200 --> 00:13:59.070
In a desperate attempt to restore order, the
king re-appointed Necker.

00:13:59.070 --> 00:14:03.959
But the finance minister refused to work with
the National Assembly and was unable to stem

00:14:03.959 --> 00:14:05.880
the flow of rebellion.

00:14:05.880 --> 00:14:10.240
On July 19th the king rode through Paris in
a carriage along with key members of the National

00:14:10.240 --> 00:14:12.080
Assembly, including Robespierre.

00:14:12.080 --> 00:14:17.400
Rather than crying out ‘Long live the king!’,
the people called out ‘Long live liberty!

00:14:17.400 --> 00:14:18.680
Long live the nation!’

00:14:18.680 --> 00:14:23.640
Louis tried to placate the crowds telling
them that he had ordered the troops to withdraw.

00:14:23.640 --> 00:14:30.410
The 150,000-armed citizens who flooded the
streets took it as too little too late.

00:14:30.410 --> 00:14:33.910
Across the country armed mobs were taking
to the streets, with many of them seizing

00:14:33.910 --> 00:14:35.950
control of their city governments.

00:14:35.950 --> 00:14:39.959
Starving people broke into granaries and the
estates of their landlords, helping themselves

00:14:39.959 --> 00:14:42.140
to food and provisions.

00:14:42.140 --> 00:14:46.459
While other members of the National Assembly
expressed concern at the growing chaos, Robespierre

00:14:46.459 --> 00:14:50.510
saw insurrection as the natural expression
of the people’s will.

00:14:50.510 --> 00:14:54.820
It was those who opposed revolution who were
the real threat.

00:14:54.820 --> 00:15:00.760
He became fanatical in his resolve to weed
out any and all who showed dissent to the

00:15:00.760 --> 00:15:01.760
apparent will of the people.

00:15:01.760 --> 00:15:12.209
The People Speak

00:15:12.209 --> 00:15:15.970
Meanwhile the National Assembly began working
on a new constitution.

00:15:15.970 --> 00:15:21.060
The Declaration of the Rights of Man and the
citizen was voted on August 26th.

00:15:21.060 --> 00:15:25.960
The king didn’t respond to the declaration
until October, by which time the riot in the

00:15:25.960 --> 00:15:27.410
streets were ongoing.

00:15:27.410 --> 00:15:30.990
He expressed concerns at a number of the articles
in the declaration.

00:15:30.990 --> 00:15:36.790
At the same time, he called the elite Flanders
Regiment to Versailles to provide extra protection.

00:15:36.790 --> 00:15:41.150
On the morning of October 5th, a large group
of market women marched on Versailles to demand

00:15:41.150 --> 00:15:42.440
flour and grain.

00:15:42.440 --> 00:15:47.290
They were met at the gates by Robespierre,
who while showing empathy for their situation,

00:15:47.290 --> 00:15:48.820
advised caution.

00:15:48.820 --> 00:15:51.779
He managed to negotiate for a single woman
to meet the king.

00:15:51.779 --> 00:15:55.420
Louis agreed to allow the release of two stores
of grains.

00:15:55.420 --> 00:15:59.070
But, again, it was too little too late.

00:15:59.070 --> 00:16:03.329
By evening a massive crowd had gathered at
Versailles, many of them armed.

00:16:03.329 --> 00:16:05.311
They lingered through the night.

00:16:05.311 --> 00:16:09.120
Then, early on the morning of October 6th,
a group of them managed to break into the

00:16:09.120 --> 00:16:10.120
Queen’s bedroom.

00:16:10.120 --> 00:16:13.080
Marie managed to escape but two of her guards
were killed.

00:16:13.080 --> 00:16:17.209
The royal couple were forced to leave the
palace and seek refuge in an unused palace

00:16:17.209 --> 00:16:18.990
in Paris, the Tuileries.

00:16:18.990 --> 00:16:22.310
They were followed by a crowd of 60,000.

00:16:22.310 --> 00:16:26.740
The king and queen spent the next few months
as virtual prisoners in the palace at Tuileries.

00:16:26.740 --> 00:16:31.200
Power rested with the National Assembly, among
which Robespierre’s influence was ever more

00:16:31.200 --> 00:16:32.200
prominent.

00:16:32.200 --> 00:16:36.140
Over the next year, the Assembly worked towards
a constitutional monarchy.

00:16:36.140 --> 00:16:39.130
In June 1791, the king had had enough.

00:16:39.130 --> 00:16:43.089
Along with his wife, he disguised himself
as a servant and fled in a carriage.

00:16:43.089 --> 00:16:47.130
He left behind a document which clearly denounced
the National Assembly.

00:16:47.130 --> 00:16:50.839
The carriage only got 160 miles out of Paris
when it was stopped and the king and queen

00:16:50.839 --> 00:16:51.839
taken under guard back to Tuileries.

00:16:51.839 --> 00:16:52.839
Causing Division

00:16:52.839 --> 00:17:06.459
The night after the king’s attempt to flee
the country, Robespierre gave an impassioned

00:17:06.459 --> 00:17:11.409
speech in which he stated that the deadliest
enemies of the French were not the Austrians,

00:17:11.409 --> 00:17:17.850
who threatened war, but counter revolutionary
forces within France itself.

00:17:17.850 --> 00:17:21.539
The king should also be counted among those
enemies of the nation.

00:17:21.539 --> 00:17:24.589
His speech broke the assembly in two.

00:17:24.589 --> 00:17:29.700
On the one side were those who clung to the
idea of a constitutional monarchy while those

00:17:29.700 --> 00:17:33.690
who sided with Robespierre were in favor of
republicanism.

00:17:33.690 --> 00:17:37.130
Robespierre began to call for the public trial
of the king.

00:17:37.130 --> 00:17:42.669
On July 17th, a group of petitioners who supported
the call were confronted by National Guardsmen.

00:17:42.669 --> 00:17:45.330
In the melee that followed fifty of them were
killed.

00:17:45.330 --> 00:17:51.669
The French constitution was completed in September,
1791, effectively putting and end to the work

00:17:51.669 --> 00:17:52.820
of the assembly.

00:17:52.820 --> 00:17:57.440
Robespierre returned to Arras, where he was
welcomed as a hero of the people.

00:17:57.440 --> 00:17:59.869
Meanwhile the king had declared war on Austria.

00:17:59.869 --> 00:18:04.450
Robespierre spoke out against the war, stating
that it was not in the interests of the people

00:18:04.450 --> 00:18:09.799
and he feared that it would galvanize them
around the king and thus destroy the revolution.

00:18:09.799 --> 00:18:15.409
During the spring of 1792, there were vocal
calls for the creation of a French Republic.

00:18:15.409 --> 00:18:20.029
Robespierre, however, had changed his tune
and was now in favour of a constitutional

00:18:20.029 --> 00:18:21.029
monarchy.

00:18:21.029 --> 00:18:24.660
However, when a large protest outside the
Tuileries on the third anniversary of the

00:18:24.660 --> 00:18:31.019
tennis court oath turned nasty, he found himself
in a stand-off with the king’s key enforcer,

00:18:31.019 --> 00:18:35.750
General Lafayette, who stood ready to put
down the marchers forcibly.

00:18:35.750 --> 00:18:39.090
Protests at the Tuileries continued, culminating
in the king and queen being forced to flee

00:18:39.090 --> 00:18:41.720
and seek protection from the National Assembly.

00:18:41.720 --> 00:18:44.190
More than a thousand people were killed that
night.

00:18:44.190 --> 00:18:49.460
In its wake, the monarchy was officially dissolved
and the royal family were taken into custody

00:18:49.460 --> 00:18:54.309
as prisoners of the state.

00:18:54.309 --> 00:19:01.840
Ominous Power

00:19:01.840 --> 00:19:06.700
Following these events Robespierre was elected
to the Insurrection Commune, which was the

00:19:06.700 --> 00:19:09.350
governing body which now kept order in Paris.

00:19:09.350 --> 00:19:14.529
He oversaw a period of interrogation of royalists
for a raft of suspected crimes against the

00:19:14.529 --> 00:19:15.529
state.

00:19:15.529 --> 00:19:20.429
Many of these royalist prisoners were pulled
from their prison cells by mobs and massacred.

00:19:20.429 --> 00:19:24.230
Others were simply handed to vengeful mobs
after mock trials.

00:19:24.230 --> 00:19:30.739
In the first week of September, 1792 around
1,400 people were killed by such mobs.

00:19:30.739 --> 00:19:35.190
Robespierre insisted that the Commune also
investigate counter-revolutionary activities.

00:19:35.190 --> 00:19:39.100
Soon it had condemned 28 people to death by
beheading.

00:19:39.100 --> 00:19:44.039
In the midst of this carnage, elections were
held for a new constitutional assembly.

00:19:44.039 --> 00:19:46.379
Robespierre was elected as a first deputy.

00:19:46.379 --> 00:19:51.700
Still, there were those within the Assembly
who objected to his violent methods of enforcement.

00:19:51.700 --> 00:19:56.759
Through force of argument he had them side-lined,
winning the day with his conviction that the

00:19:56.759 --> 00:20:01.679
end justified the means, no matter how violent
that means became.

00:20:01.679 --> 00:20:06.590
The trial of the king began on December 26,
1792.

00:20:06.590 --> 00:20:09.740
Three weeks later he was found unanimously
guilty.

00:20:09.740 --> 00:20:13.769
Robespierre himself summed up the will of
the times . . . “It is with regret that

00:20:13.769 --> 00:20:20.090
I must pronounce the fatal truth; the king
must die so that the country may live!”

00:20:20.090 --> 00:20:25.090
France executed its king of January 21st,
1793.

00:20:25.090 --> 00:20:38.429
Robespierre did not attend the occasion.

00:20:38.429 --> 00:20:39.429
The Reign of Terror

00:20:39.429 --> 00:20:43.090
On July 20th, 1793, Robespierre was elected
to the Committee of Public Safety, which had

00:20:43.090 --> 00:20:45.299
been established a few months earlier.

00:20:45.299 --> 00:20:48.559
The Committee began to take action against
federalist revolutionists.

00:20:48.559 --> 00:20:54.320
Mass executions were ordered in Lyons, which
was a hotbed of royalist sympathy.

00:20:54.320 --> 00:20:58.500
Revolts were breaking out all over, leading
the Convention to declare terror ‘the order

00:20:58.500 --> 00:20:59.500
of the day’.

00:20:59.500 --> 00:21:04.009
On September 17th, they passed laws allowing
them to put to death anyone who was implicated

00:21:04.009 --> 00:21:06.039
as a supporter of tyranny.

00:21:06.039 --> 00:21:09.940
Caught up in the net of the reign of terror
was Marie Antoinette.

00:21:09.940 --> 00:21:14.779
After a sham trial, she was sent to the guillotine
on October 16, 1793.

00:21:14.779 --> 00:21:20.950
Robespierre now set his sights on his former
National Assembly opposers, the Girondists.

00:21:20.950 --> 00:21:25.149
They were duly tried and found guilty and
sent to the guillotine.

00:21:25.149 --> 00:21:29.330
Controlling the executions was Robespierre
who famously declared . . .

00:21:29.330 --> 00:21:35.700
To punish the oppressors of humanity is clemency;
to forgive them is barbarity.

00:21:35.700 --> 00:21:40.360
Those within the assembly who opposed Robespierre
found themselves facing the guillotine themselves.

00:21:40.360 --> 00:21:42.980
Before long he had absolute power within the
Committee.

00:21:42.980 --> 00:21:49.350
He had become a virtual dictator, literally
with the power of life or death within his

00:21:49.350 --> 00:21:50.350
hands.

00:21:50.350 --> 00:21:53.309
Along with his immense power, Robespierre
grew increasingly paranoid.

00:21:53.309 --> 00:21:57.039
There was an attempt on his life in May, 1793.

00:21:57.039 --> 00:21:59.590
The following month he was elected President
of the Convention.

00:21:59.590 --> 00:22:04.279
He immediately enacted changes to allow him
to condemn even more people to death.

00:22:04.279 --> 00:22:09.500
Trials were reduced to mere condemnations
and all accused were denied legal representation.

00:22:09.500 --> 00:22:14.720
He even created a new category of criminal
called ‘enemy of the people’.

00:22:14.720 --> 00:22:19.630
This blanket term could cover anything from
serving sour wine to sending a letter to England,

00:22:19.630 --> 00:22:25.649
yet the punishment was always the same – death
by guillotine.

00:22:25.649 --> 00:22:34.299
Justice of the Blade

00:22:34.299 --> 00:22:37.059
By this time Robespierre had gone too far.

00:22:37.059 --> 00:22:40.600
The people were beginning to reject his despotic
rule of terror.

00:22:40.600 --> 00:22:44.679
His political enemies orchestrated a falsified
letter which appeared to implicate Robespierre

00:22:44.679 --> 00:22:46.309
in an attempted coup d'etat.

00:22:46.309 --> 00:22:51.509
He defended himself against charges of dictatorship
in a two-hour speech, in the process warning

00:22:51.509 --> 00:22:54.379
against a conspiracy that was being hatched
against the Republic.

00:22:54.379 --> 00:22:56.460
But it was to no avail.

00:22:56.460 --> 00:23:00.610
The next day he was arrested only to be freed
shortly thereafter by troops from the Paris

00:23:00.610 --> 00:23:01.610
Commune.

00:23:01.610 --> 00:23:05.479
Robespierre and his defenders found themselves
holed up at the Hotel de Ville.

00:23:05.479 --> 00:23:09.229
They were declared outlaws by the Convention,
which meant that when caught they could be

00:23:09.229 --> 00:23:10.799
put to death immediately.

00:23:10.799 --> 00:23:15.309
When the Convention forces closed in on the
hotel, Robespierre and those who were with

00:23:15.309 --> 00:23:17.190
him all tried to commit suicide.

00:23:17.190 --> 00:23:21.499
Some of them succeeded but Robespierre’s
attempt to blow his brains out only managed

00:23:21.499 --> 00:23:23.649
to shatter his lower jaw.

00:23:23.649 --> 00:23:27.999
With blood pouring from his face, Robespierre
was laid on a table in the room of the Committee

00:23:27.999 --> 00:23:32.739
for Public Safety before being transferred
to the cell that had housed Marie Antoinette

00:23:32.739 --> 00:23:35.059
prior to her date with the guillotine.

00:23:35.059 --> 00:23:40.340
The end came for Robespierre on July 28th,
1794 when he became the final victim of his

00:23:40.340 --> 00:23:41.669
reign of terror.

00:23:41.669 --> 00:23:45.480
Seconds before the blade fell, the executioner
ripped off the bandage that was keeping his

00:23:45.480 --> 00:23:49.519
jaw together, causing him to let out an almighty
scream.

00:23:49.519 --> 00:23:54.679
It was soon silenced by the deadly blade,
finally ending the carnage that Robespierre’s

00:23:54.679 --> 00:24:29.929
warped view of justice had wrought.

